  "hero.kicker": "How Things Work · Semiconductors · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Why Faster Chips Keep Getting Harder",
  "hero.sub": "From a piece of silicon that conducts,<br>to a factory where a billion dollars barely buys a foothold",
  "hero.lead": "For fifty years, chips kept getting faster, more power-efficient, and cheaper every few years, as if that were a law of nature. It never was: every step came from engineers finding a way around a physical or economic wall. This essay asks one question: <strong>why does making a faster chip keep getting harder?</strong> We start from how silicon can be switched on and off, and climb one level at a time: diodes, transistors, scaling rules, lithography, node names, transistor geometry, wires, yield and cost, ending where the industry actually stands in 2026. Each chapter has a model you can push around, built on the real equations of the field scaled down to fit on a page.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The models are standard textbook models (sources are given at the end of each chapter and in the footer), simplified so you can see one mechanism clearly—their numbers are model outputs, not measurements from any fab line. Wherever real measurements or company figures appear, I state the source and date. The final chapter is the part that goes stale: each item is dated and tagged as in production, demonstrated, or a company claim. Nothing here is investment advice.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "When Silicon Conducts",
  "ch1.law": "Pure silicon barely conducts; add a trace of impurity and its conductivity jumps by hundreds of thousands of times. Every chip rests on being able to decide, spot by spot, where electricity flows.",
  "ch1.p1": "Copper is packed with electrons free to move at any moment, so copper always conducts. Glass has none, so it never does. Silicon sits in between: its electrons are locked in chemical bonds between atoms, and breaking one free takes about 1.12 eV of energy—the <strong>band gap</strong>. A freed electron can wander through the crystal, and the empty spot it leaves behind (a <strong>hole</strong>) can move too. Both carry current.",
  "ch1.p2": "That energy comes from heat. At temperature T, the probability that a state is occupied by an electron follows the <strong>Fermi–Dirac distribution</strong> f(E) = 1/(1 + e<sup>(E−E<sub>F</sub>)/kT</sup>); when an energy sits many kT above the Fermi level E<sub>F</sub>, this simplifies to the Boltzmann factor e<sup>−(E−E<sub>F</sub>)/kT</sup>. At room temperature kT is only 0.026 eV, while crossing half the band gap takes about 22 kT, so the odds are tiny—though not zero. Multiplying that probability by the number of available states in the conduction band gives the intrinsic carrier concentration n<sub>i</sub> = √(N<sub>c</sub>N<sub>v</sub>)·e<sup>−E<sub>g</sub>/2kT</sup>. For where the Boltzmann factor comes from, see <a href=\"../../../physics/thermal/kinetic-theory/\">The Microscopic Picture of Gas Pressure</a>; for why energies come in discrete levels, see <a href=\"../../../physics/modern/quantum-basics/\">Quantum Basics</a>.",
  "ch1.model.title": "How many conducting electrons are in silicon",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag the temperature and doping concentration, and choose phosphorus (extra electrons, n-type) or boron (extra holes, p-type). Top: the band diagram—the closer the Fermi level (gold line) moves toward the conduction band, the more electrons there are; the curve on the right is the Fermi–Dirac distribution. Bottom: electron and hole concentrations versus temperature, with the vertical line at your chosen temperature.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Top: silicon band diagram and Fermi–Dirac distribution. Bottom: logarithmic plot of electron and hole concentrations versus temperature",
  "ch1.ctl.T": "Temperature",
  "ch1.ctl.N": "Doping concentration",
