  "ch1.leg.ni": "Intrinsic nᵢ",
  "ch1.say.intrinsic": "Undoped: electrons and holes are equal at <em>{ni}</em> per cm³, all created by thermal energy. Since 1 cm³ of silicon holds 5×10²² atoms, only one atom in {per} contributes a free electron.",
  "ch1.say.extrinsic": "Doped with {N} cm⁻³ of {dop}: only one silicon atom in {per} is replaced, yet the {maj} concentration already equals the dopant concentration—{x} times the intrinsic level {ni}. You decide what conducts, not the temperature.",
  "ch1.say.near": "At {T} K the silicon is nearing its intrinsic temperature ({Ti} K): thermally generated carriers are catching up with the dopants, and doping is starting to lose control.",
  "ch1.say.hot": "At {T} K—above the intrinsic temperature of {Ti} K—heat creates more electrons and holes than the dopants supply, erasing the difference between n-type and p-type. At this temperature, a chip stops working as a chip.",
  "ch1.dop.n": "phosphorus",
  "ch1.dop.p": "boron",
  "ch1.maj.n": "electron",
  "ch1.maj.p": "hole",
  "ch1.p3": "At 300 K, the model gives an intrinsic carrier concentration of 9.8×10⁹ cm⁻³ (measured literature values lie between 9.65×10⁹ and 1.0×10¹⁰). Pure silicon then has a resistivity of about 3.5×10⁵ Ω·cm. Doping it with 10¹⁶ cm⁻³ of phosphorus—replacing just one silicon atom in 5 million—drops the resistivity to 0.56 Ω·cm, a change of roughly <strong>600,000 times</strong>. That is what makes a semiconductor useful: tiny amounts of impurity placed in chosen regions turn an insulator into a conductor.",
  "ch1.p4": "Temperature is the other hand on the wheel. Near room temperature, every 9 K rise roughly doubles the intrinsic concentration. In silicon doped to 10¹⁶ cm⁻³, thermal carriers match the dopants around 649 K (about 376 °C), after which doping loses control. Chips run well below that, but this same exponential sensitivity returns in Chapter 3 as leakage.",
  "ch1.limit": "Model simplifications: assumes complete dopant ionization (which breaks down below ~100 K), omits heavy-doping band-gap narrowing, and evaluates Caughey–Thomas mobility at 300 K rather than scaling it with temperature. N<sub>c</sub>, N<sub>v</sub> and E<sub>g</sub>(T) are from Sze & Ng, Physics of Semiconductor Devices, 3rd ed.",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "The pn Junction: A One-Way Gate",
  "ch2.law": "Press p-type and n-type silicon together and a built-in voltage barrier forms at the boundary; every ~60 mV of forward bias multiplies the current crossing it by ten.",
  "ch2.p1": "The n-side has plenty of electrons; the p-side has plenty of holes. When they meet, electrons diffuse into the p-side and holes diffuse into the n-side, neutralizing each other near the boundary and leaving behind a layer stripped of free carriers, holding only fixed charged dopant ions: the <strong>depletion region</strong>. Those ions create an electric field that pushes carriers back until diffusion and drift balance. The voltage across that field is the <strong>built-in potential</strong> V<sub>bi</sub> = (kT/q)·ln(N<sub>A</sub>N<sub>D</sub>/n<sub>i</sub>²), bringing back n<sub>i</sub> from Chapter 1.",
  "ch2.p2": "Apply a forward voltage and the barrier shrinks, so the carriers surmounting it grow with the Boltzmann factor e<sup>qV/kT</sup>; apply a reverse voltage and the barrier rises, leaving almost no current. That gives the <strong>Shockley diode equation</strong> I = I<sub>s</sub>(e<sup>qV/kT</sup> − 1). To picture the field inside the depletion region, compare <a href=\"../../../physics/electromagnetism/electric-fields/\">Electric Fields</a>.",
  "ch2.model.title": "A silicon pn junction",
  "ch2.model.how": "Drag the doping on each side and the applied voltage (positive is forward bias). Top: the band diagram across the junction, with the depletion region shaded gray. Bottom: |current| versus voltage on a log scale, with the gold dot at the current voltage.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Top: pn junction energy bands and depletion region. Bottom: logarithmic plot of diode current versus voltage",
  "ch2.ctl.na": "p-side doping N<sub>A</sub>",
  "ch2.ctl.nd": "n-side doping N<sub>D</sub>",
  "ch2.ctl.v": "Applied voltage",
