  "ch2.unit.nm": "{v} nm",
  "ch2.unit.um": "{v} µm",
  "ch2.unit.kvcm": "{v} kV/cm",
  "ch2.cv.x": "Position",
  "ch2.cv.E": "Electron energy",
  "ch2.cv.p": "p-side",
  "ch2.cv.n": "n-side",
  "ch2.cv.v": "Voltage (V)",
  "ch2.cv.i": "|Current| (A)",
  "ch2.say.fwd": "Forward bias {v} V: the barrier drops from {vbi} V to {vb} V, and current is {i}. Another ~60 mV makes it ten times larger.",
  "ch2.say.zero": "Zero bias: diffusion and electric field cancel, with no net current. The barrier is {vbi} V high and the depletion region is {w} wide.",
  "ch2.say.rev": "Reverse bias {v} V: the barrier rises to {vb} V, the depletion region widens to {w}, and only {i} of leakage gets through. The gate is shut, though not airtight.",
  "ch2.p3": "At the default settings (10¹⁷ cm⁻³ p-side, 10¹⁶ cm⁻³ n-side), the built-in potential is 0.775 V and the depletion region is 332 nm wide; applying 2 V of reverse bias widens it to 628 nm. At room temperature, each tenfold jump in forward current takes ln10·kT/q = <strong>59.5 mV</strong>, rising to 69.4 mV at 350 K. That number depends on neither doping, area, nor device details—only on the Boltzmann factor.",
  "ch2.p4": "Remember this 60 mV. In the next chapter it returns as the “subthreshold swing”—and becomes the reason chip voltages have barely budged for twenty years.",
  "ch2.limit": "Model simplifications: abrupt junction, depletion approximation, and ideal Shockley diode (no generation–recombination inside the depletion region, no high-level injection, no series resistance). Minority-carrier lifetime is set to 1 µs, which scales I<sub>s</sub> without changing the 59.5 mV/decade slope.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "Transistors: Voltage-Controlled Switches",
  "ch3.law": "A transistor does not snap off cleanly: each drop of one “swing” in gate voltage cuts leakage by only a factor of ten, and at room temperature that swing cannot be less than 60 mV.",
  "ch3.p1": "A MOSFET adds a gate to Chapter 2's structure: a metal or polysilicon electrode separated from the silicon by a thin insulator. When gate voltage exceeds the <strong>threshold voltage</strong> V<sub>t</sub>, the silicon surface beneath inverts and forms a conducting channel between source and drain, closing the switch. <a href=\"../../../computing/systems/switches-that-compute/#ch1\">How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic</a> shows how wiring such switches together builds any logic; here we examine how good a single switch is.",
  "ch3.p2": "A good switch needs two things: high current when on (I<sub>on</sub>, which sets speed) and tiny current when off (I<sub>off</sub>, which sets standby power). The catch is that below V<sub>t</sub>, current does not drop to zero—it falls exponentially just like a diode, because electrons climbing the source barrier still obey the Boltzmann factor. Reducing gate voltage by S = n·(kT/q)·ln10 cuts current tenfold. S is the <strong>subthreshold swing</strong>, where n ≥ 1 measures how completely the gate controls the channel potential; at n = 1, S equals Chapter 2's 59.5 mV.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Transfer curve of a single transistor",
  "ch3.model.how": "Drag the threshold voltage, supply voltage, temperature and control factor n. The curve shows drain current versus gate voltage on a log vertical axis: the straight slope is the subthreshold region, set by S. The red dot marks the off-state (gate at 0 V) and the gold dot marks the on-state (gate at supply voltage).",
  "ch3.model.aria": "Logarithmic plot of transistor drain current versus gate voltage, marking off-state and on-state currents",
  "ch3.ctl.vt": "Threshold V<sub>t</sub>",
  "ch3.ctl.vdd": "Supply V<sub>DD</sub>",
  "ch3.ctl.T": "Temperature",
  "ch3.ctl.n": "Control factor n",
  "ch3.read.s": "Subthreshold swing",
  "ch3.read.ion": "On-current I<sub>on</sub>",
  "ch3.read.ioff": "Off-current I<sub>off</sub>",
  "ch3.read.ratio": "On/off ratio",
  "ch3.unit.mvdec": "{v} mV/dec",
  "ch3.unit.ua": "{v} µA/µm",
  "ch3.unit.na": "{v} nA/µm",
  "ch3.unit.pa": "{v} pA/µm",
  "ch3.unit.k": "{v} K",
  "ch3.cv.vg": "Gate voltage (V)",
  "ch3.cv.id": "Drain current (A/µm)",
  "ch3.cv.on": "On",
  "ch3.cv.off": "Off",
  "ch3.say.ok": "On/off ratio {r}: on-current exceeds off-current by this factor, passing this page's illustrative 10⁵ bar for a usable switch. Swing is {s} mV/dec, {ideal}",
  "ch3.say.leaky": "On/off ratio is only {r}, below this page's 10⁵ bar. Either the threshold is too close to 0 V or the supply leaves too little headroom: on and off are separated by only {dec} decades.",
  "ch3.say.ideal": "right at the room-temperature thermal limit.",
  "ch3.say.nonideal": "short of the n = 1 limit of {lim}: the gate does not have complete control over the channel.",
  "ch3.p3": "At the defaults (V<sub>t</sub> = 0.3 V, n = 1.2, 300 K), the swing is 71 mV/dec, off-current is about 1.0 nA/µm, on-current is 844 µA/µm, and the on/off ratio is 8.4×10⁵. Now try saving power: dynamic power scales with voltage squared, so drop V<sub>DD</sub> from 0.75 V to 0.45 V. On-current plunges to 100 µA/µm, making the chip more than 8× slower. To recover speed you would lower V<sub>t</sub>—but cutting V<sub>t</sub> from 0.3 V to 0.2 V multiplies off-state leakage by 24.",
  "ch3.p4": "That is the deadlock. Given a leakage budget of, say, 1 nA/µm, V<sub>t</sub> cannot drop below about 0.30 V—and even with ideal gate control (n = 1), it cannot go below about 0.25 V. Because V<sub>t</sub> is pinned, V<sub>DD</sub> is pinned too, since the on-state needs headroom above V<sub>t</sub>. This floor comes from kT/q: it is thermodynamics, not imperfect manufacturing. Warm the same transistor to 350 K and leakage jumps 5.4×.",
  "ch3.limit": "Model simplifications: uses the EKV interpolation formula (Enz, Krummenacher & Vittoz 1995) in long-channel saturation, without velocity saturation or short-channel effects (added in Chapter 8). β is scaled so on-currents land in the hundreds of µA/µm—an illustrative scale that affects absolute current, not the on/off ratio or swing. Steep-slope devices to beat 60 mV/dec (tunnel FETs, negative-capacitance FETs) remain in research.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "Shrinking Used to Be Free",
  "ch4.law": "When dimensions and voltages shrink together, transistors get faster, denser and more efficient while power per unit area stays flat; once voltage stops shrinking, that free lunch ends.",
  "ch4.p1": "In 1974, Robert Dennard and colleagues at IBM wrote down a scaling table: divide transistor length, width, oxide thickness and voltage by κ, and multiply doping by κ. Each transistor's capacitance falls by 1/κ, delay falls by 1/κ, power falls by 1/κ², and κ² times as many transistors fit in the same area. Multiply them together and <strong>power density stays constant</strong>. That is <strong>Dennard scaling</strong>. For thirty years, every node shrank dimensions by roughly 0.7× (κ = √2): twice as many transistors in the same die area, ~40% higher clock speed, and no extra heat per square millimeter.",
  "ch4.p2": "One line in Dennard's table is easy to miss: voltage also had to be divided by κ. Chapter 3 showed why voltage eventually hit a wall. The model below plugs Chapter 3's transistor into Dennard's table under three voltage strategies.",
  "ch4.model.title": "How power density changes across generations",
  "ch4.model.how": "Drag the generation slider; each generation multiplies all lengths by 0.71. Pick one of three voltage strategies, and toggle whether clock frequency keeps rising. Top: power per unit area relative to generation 0 on a log scale, split into switching power (blue) and leakage (red). Bottom: measured clock speeds of real processors from 1971 to 2021.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Top: stacked power density versus process generation. Bottom: scatter plot of historical microprocessor clock frequencies",
  "ch4.ctl.gen": "Generation",
