  "ch4.cv.f": "Clock frequency (MHz)",
  "ch4.leg.dyn": "Switching power",
  "ch4.leg.leak": "Leakage",
  "ch4.leg.data": "Real processors (Rupp)",
  "ch4.say.flat": "Generation {g} ({L}): power per unit area is {x}× generation 0—practically flat. That is Dennard scaling's free lunch.",
  "ch4.say.leak": "Generation {g} ({L}): threshold voltage has fallen to {vt} V, leakage now takes {share} of total power, and power density hits {x}×. Scaling threshold voltage blindly melts the chip with leaky switches.",
  "ch4.say.hot": "Generation {g} ({L}): without voltage scaling, power density climbs to {x}×. Cooling that much more heat per square millimeter is impossible.",
  "ch4.say.cool": "Generation {g} ({L}): holding clock frequency flat keeps power density at {x}× (leakage {share}). The trade-off: extra transistors go into more cores rather than a faster single core.",
  "ch4.p3": "Pick “Dennard” with frequency scaling on: for the first three generations, power density barely moves. But threshold voltage shrinks too, so by generation 5 (~180 nm) leakage already accounts for 63% of total power and power density jumps to 2.7×; by generation 6 it hits 10.9×. Pick “Fixed voltage” with frequency scaling: power density doubles every generation, reaching 256× by generation 8.",
  "ch4.p4": "What happened in reality was the third option: supply voltage stalled around 1 V and threshold voltage around 0.3 V. Keep raising clock frequency anyway and generation 8 hits 10.7× power density; hold frequency flat and power density stays near baseline. The real processor data below records that pivot: in this dataset, top clock speeds stayed under 540 MHz before 2000, surged to 3.65 GHz in 2000–2005, and peaked at 4.7 GHz all the way through 2021. Industry spent the extra transistors on multiple cores, larger caches and specialized accelerators—and coined “dark silicon” for transistors that cannot all be powered on at once.",
  "ch4.limit": "Model notes: generation 0 starts at 1 µm, 5 V, threshold 0.75 V, 25 MHz—round numbers chosen to set the initial leakage-to-switching ratio, not a specific historical chip; “Stop at 1 V” illustrates the trend rather than fitting each node. Clock data is from Karl Rupp's 50 Years of Microprocessor Trend Data (CC BY 4.0; pre-2010 compiled by Horowitz et al.), where each dot is a processor. Post-2007 leakage fixes such as high-κ metal gates and FinFETs are omitted here.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "Printing Lines Finer Than Light",
  "ch5.law": "The finest feature an optical tool can print is k₁·λ/NA; physics caps k₁ at 0.25, so shrinking further demands shorter wavelengths, higher numerical aperture, or splitting one layer across multiple exposures.",
  "ch5.p1": "Chip patterns are printed photographically: light shines through a patterned mask, passes through projection optics that shrink it 4×, and lands on a silicon wafer coated with photoresist, which changes chemically where exposed so a developer can wash away one side. The limit is diffraction. A regular grating of lines (pitch P) splits light into diffraction orders at angles sinθ = mλ/P, the same physics as <a href=\"../../../physics/waves/interference/\">Interference</a> and <a href=\"../../../physics/modern/double-slit/\">The Double-Slit Experiment</a>.",
  "ch5.p2": "A lens only collects light with sinθ up to its <strong>numerical aperture</strong> (NA). Capture only the 0th order and the wafer sees uniform brightness—nothing prints. You need at least one 1st-order beam to interfere with the 0th order and form bright and dark fringes. That is Abbe imaging, and it yields the Rayleigh equation CD = k₁·λ/NA. Tilting the illumination so the 0th and 1st orders sit on opposite edges of the pupil pushes the minimum pitch down to λ/(2NA), corresponding to half-pitch k₁ = 0.25.",
  "ch5.model.title": "Aerial image of a line/space grating",
  "ch5.model.how": "Pick a lithography tool and illumination mode, drag the target pitch, and choose how many exposures to split the layer across. Top: the intensity profile from a single exposure (1D Abbe imaging summed across source points). Bottom: the final lines printed on the wafer, colored by exposure pass.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Top: aerial image intensity of mask lines through the projection lens. Bottom: resulting lines printed on the wafer",
