  "ch5.cv.none": "Cannot print",
  "ch5.say.ok": "Exposure pitch is {ep} nm (k₁ = {k1}, contrast {c}): printable. {splitNote}",
  "ch5.say.weak": "Exposure pitch {ep} nm (k₁ = {k1}) gives contrast of only {c}: bright and dark blur together so the resist cannot separate lines cleanly. Try off-axis illumination or splitting into more exposures.",
  "ch5.say.dark": "Exposure pitch {ep} nm is too tight for this lens to capture any 1st-order diffracted light: the wafer sees uniform light and nothing prints.",
  "ch5.split.note1": "",
  "ch5.split.note2": "The cost: this layer now takes two passes (or self-aligned sidewall spacers), doubling process steps and overlay error.",
  "ch5.split.note4": "The cost: quadruple patterning—repeated cycles of deposition and etch for one layer, compounding cost and alignment error at every step.",
  "ch5.p3": "In the model, ArF immersion (NA 1.35) with dipole illumination drops below the contrast threshold at a half-pitch of 38.0 nm, or k₁ = 0.266—matching ASML's “≤ 38 nm” spec for the NXT:2150i. On-axis illumination stalls around 50 nm half-pitch. The hard theoretical floor k₁ = 0.25 sits at 35.7 nm: the end of the line for 193 nm light. “Immersion” fills the gap between lens and wafer with water (refractive index ~1.44), letting NA exceed 1 so the lens catches steeper rays.",
  "ch5.p4": "Yet transistors kept shrinking. A “3 nm” node from 2022 uses a tightest metal pitch around 24 nm (Chapter 7). ArF immersion prints at best a 76 nm pitch in one pass; splitting into two passes gives 48 nm per pass, still unprintable; only splitting into four passes (96 nm per pass) works. That is <strong>multi-patterning</strong>—how 7 nm chips were built, at the price of turning one layer into many fab steps. EUV cuts λ from 193 nm to 13.5 nm, reaching ~21.7 nm pitch in a single pass in the model; even so, IRDS 2025 notes that 24 nm pitch in practice mostly uses EUV at 46 nm pitch plus double patterning because of photoresist limits (next chapter). High-NA EUV lifts NA from 0.33 to 0.55, pushing the model's single-exposure half-pitch down to 6.5 nm. But depth of focus scales as λ/NA²: raising NA from 0.33 to 0.55 at the same wavelength leaves only 36% of the depth of focus, forcing flatter wafers and thinner resists.",
  "ch5.limit": "Model simplifications: scalar, thin-mask Abbe imaging with a 1D source—no polarization, 3D mask effects, resist diffusion, or stochastic blur, and schematic source shapes. The printable contrast threshold of 0.3 is my rule of thumb. ASML's rated resolutions for EUV (13 nm at 0.33 NA; 8 nm at 0.55 NA) are looser than the optical limit here because of resist and photon noise (next chapter).",

  /* ───── 06 ───── */
  "ch6.title": "EUV: Why One Scanner Is So Hard to Build",
  "ch6.law": "13.5 nm light is absorbed by nearly all matter, so it must travel through vacuum on mirrors; each mirror reflects only ~70%, leaving just a few percent by the time the beam hits the wafer.",
  "ch6.p1": "At a wavelength of 13.5 nm, each photon carries hc/λ = 91.8 eV—14.3 times the energy of a 193 nm photon (6.42 eV). Air, glass, and almost any solid absorb it immediately, so refractive lenses are impossible: the entire beam path must sit in vacuum and use mirrors. Ordinary mirrors absorb it too, so EUV optics rely on Bragg reflectors coated with more than a hundred alternating layers of molybdenum and silicon, each a few nanometers thick, adding faint reflections in phase. Measured reflectivity at 13.5 nm is about 70% (theoretical ceiling ~75%). For photon energy calculations, see <a href=\"../../../physics/modern/photoelectric-effect/\">The Photoelectric Effect</a>.",
  "ch6.p2": "Generating the light is equally extreme: a stream of ~25 µm molten tin droplets flies through a vacuum chamber at 50,000 drops per second. Each droplet is flattened by a weak pre-pulse laser and then blasted by a high-power CO₂ laser into a ~200,000 °C plasma that radiates 13.5 nm light into a collector mirror—which a few nanometers of stray tin buildup can ruin.",
  "ch6.model.title": "Light from source to wafer",
  "ch6.model.how": "Drag the number of reflections (including the mask, which is also a mirror), mirror reflectivity, source power and exposure dose. Top: remaining power at each reflection on a log scale. Bottom: a 10×10 patch of resist pixels (adjustable width, roughly one line width), shaded by random photon arrivals.",
  "ch6.model.aria": "Top: step chart of optical power falling with each reflection. Bottom: random Poisson photon arrivals across 10×10 resist pixels",
  "ch6.ctl.mirrors": "Reflections",
