  "ch6.cv.src": "Source",
  "ch6.cv.wafer": "Wafer",
  "ch6.btn.reroll": "Re-expose",
  "ch6.say.main": "After {m} reflections only {t} survives: a {P} W source delivers just {pw} W to the wafer. Counting mirror losses alone, exposing a 300 mm wafer at {dose} mJ/cm² takes {sec} s (at most {wph} wafers/hour). Each {px} nm square pixel averages {n} photons, fluctuating by ~{noise}.",
  "ch6.say.noisy": "After {m} reflections only {t} survives. Each {px} nm square pixel averages just {n} photons, fluctuating by ~{noise}: some pixels get underexposed, leaving bridges or broken lines. Suppressing that randomness requires higher dose—which cuts wafers per hour.",
  "ch6.scale": "Shading shows each pixel's photon count relative to the mean, from darkest to brightest across {v}.",
  "ch6.p3": "At the defaults (11 reflections, 70% each, 250 W source, 20 mJ/cm²), only <strong>2.0%</strong> of the light reaches the wafer—about 4.9 W out of 250 W. Counting mirror loss alone, exposing one wafer at 20 mJ/cm² takes 2.9 s, giving a ceiling of ~1,259 wafers per hour; ASML's rated throughput for the NXE:3400C at 20 mJ/cm² is ≥170 wafers/hour. The gap comes from illuminator losses, mask absorber and pellicle losses, plus wafer stage motion and alignment overhead omitted by the model.",
  "ch6.p4": "Why not simply use a lower dose? Raise dose to 30 mJ/cm² and look at the pixel grid: a 13 nm square averages only ~3,446 EUV photons, with ~1.7% Poisson shot noise; at the same dose, 193 nm light over a 38 nm square packs roughly 420,000 photons. Because each EUV photon carries so much energy, a given dose contains far fewer photons, raising relative noise (<a href=\"../../../probability/stochastic/poisson-process/\">The Poisson Process</a>). Smaller features mean smaller pixels and fewer photons still—which is why the IRDS roadmap says stochastic defects and line-edge roughness are harder than raw resolution. Source power becomes the bottleneck for fab throughput: in its 2025 annual report, ASML noted demonstrating a 1,000 W EUV source in April 2025.",
  "ch6.limit": "Model simplifications: counts only mirror reflections and incident photon statistics. 11 reflections is my illustrative count (EUV optics typically use 8–12 mirrors plus the reflective mask); resist absorption fraction and secondary electron blur are omitted.",

  /* ───── 07 ───── */
  "ch7.title": "There Is No 3 nm Inside “3 nm”",
  "ch7.law": "Node names stopped matching any physical dimension decades ago; what sets transistor density per square millimeter is gate pitch, metal pitch and standard-cell height.",
  "ch7.p1": "Until the 1990s, a process node name roughly equaled the transistor gate length. When gate lengths slowed down, node names kept stepping down by ~0.7× per generation as marketing labels. IEEE's device roadmap (IRDS) switched to explicit geometry labels: the 2022 “3 nm” generation is written “G48M24”—meaning a 48 nm contacted gate pitch (CPP) and a 24 nm minimum metal pitch (MMP).",
  "ch7.p2": "Logic chips are tiled from standard cells. A two-input NAND gate (four transistors; see <a href=\"../../../computing/systems/switches-that-compute/#ch2\">why it is enough</a>) is 3 gate pitches wide and several metal tracks tall, so cell height = tracks × metal pitch. Multiplying width by height gives density.",
  "ch7.model.title": "How big a NAND2 gate is",
  "ch7.model.how": "Pick a node preset or drag gate pitch, metal pitch, track height and stacked device tiers yourself. The canvas draws the NAND2 cell to scale (gold vertical bars are gates; purple horizontal bars are metal tracks), with a red bar below showing the length of the node name itself.",
  "ch7.model.aria": "Scale drawing of a two-input NAND standard cell compared with the length of its node name",
  "ch7.ctl.preset": "Node",
  "ch7.preset.intel14": "Intel 14 nm (measured)",
  "ch7.preset.intel10": "Intel 10 nm (measured)",
  "ch7.preset.g48m24": "“3 nm” G48M24",
  "ch7.preset.g45m20": "“2.1 nm” G45M20",
  "ch7.preset.g42m16": "“1.5 nm” G42M16",
  "ch7.preset.g40m16t2": "“1.0 nm” 2-tier stack",
